WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.249 --> 00:00:07.483
- My name is Bryan
Stevenson, and I'm a lawyer,

00:00:07.483 --> 00:00:11.106
an advocate and an activist
for ending mass incarceration

00:00:11.106 --> 00:00:12.514
and creating racial justice in America.

00:00:12.514 --> 00:00:14.638
- [Man] Racial justice in America,

00:00:14.638 --> 00:00:16.915
creating racial justice in America.

00:00:16.915 --> 00:00:20.332
(gentle, haunting music)

00:00:39.785 --> 00:00:41.748
- [Bryan] I've been
trying to say something

00:00:41.748 --> 00:00:43.107
about our criminal justice system.

00:00:43.107 --> 00:00:45.254
Whenever you're talking about a violation

00:00:45.254 --> 00:00:48.219
of basic human rights, a
violation of basic human dignity,

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:50.062
your first requirement is to make sure

00:00:50.062 --> 00:00:51.978
people see the humanity of the people,

00:00:51.978 --> 00:00:54.978
who are experiencing these injuries.

00:00:56.461 --> 00:00:59.514
(gentle, haunting music)

00:00:59.514 --> 00:01:01.659
In this country, we
don't talk about slavery,

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:02.492
we don't talk about lynching,

00:01:02.492 --> 00:01:03.890
we don't talk about segregation

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.779
and we don't wanna talk about racial bias

00:01:06.779 --> 00:01:09.362
in our criminal justice system.

00:01:12.266 --> 00:01:14.948
If we're serious about
correcting the imbalance

00:01:14.948 --> 00:01:17.540
created by decades of
inequality and injustice,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.782
we have to do more than just be silent.

00:01:20.782 --> 00:01:22.858
(gentle, quirky music)

00:01:22.858 --> 00:01:24.531
- [Man] I've been trying
to say something about

00:01:24.531 --> 00:01:25.708
criminal justice system,
say, say something

00:01:25.708 --> 00:01:29.541
about criminal justice,
I've been, I've been--

00:01:33.504 --> 00:01:35.166
this country is very different today,

00:01:35.166 --> 00:01:37.249
than it was 40 years ago.

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:42.175
In 1972 there were 300,000
people in jails and prisons.

00:01:42.175 --> 00:01:45.374
Today there are 2.3
million, million, million,

00:01:45.374 --> 00:01:47.408
million, million, million, million.

00:01:47.408 --> 00:01:50.241
(hammering gavel)

00:01:53.063 --> 00:01:54.762
The United States now has the highest rate

00:01:54.762 --> 00:01:56.480
of incarceration in the world.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.984
We have seven million people
on probation and parole

00:01:58.984 --> 00:02:01.633
and mass incarceration,
incarceration, incarceration.

00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:04.716
It's fundamentally changed our world.

00:02:07.312 --> 00:02:09.224
In poor communities and
communities of color,

00:02:09.224 --> 00:02:11.700
there is this despair,
there is this hopelessness,

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.000
that has been shaped by these outcomes.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.497
One out of three black men
between the ages of 18 and 30

00:02:16.497 --> 00:02:19.899
is in jail, in prison,
on probation or parole.

00:02:19.899 --> 00:02:22.062
One out of, one out of, one
out of, one out of three, three, three, three,

00:02:22.062 --> 00:02:24.350
black, black, black, black,
prison, prison, prison, prison,

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:28.517
probation, probation, probation,
parole, parole, parole.

00:02:29.387 --> 00:02:30.548
I've been trying to say something

00:02:30.548 --> 00:02:34.165
about our criminal justice system.

00:02:34.165 --> 00:02:37.369
(gentle, quirky music)

00:02:37.369 --> 00:02:39.708
Our system isn't just
being shaped in these ways,

00:02:39.708 --> 00:02:41.837
that seem to be distorting around race,

00:02:41.837 --> 00:02:43.238
they're also distorted by poverty.

00:02:43.238 --> 00:02:46.068
We have a system of
justice in this country,

00:02:46.068 --> 00:02:49.742
that treats you much better
if you're rich and guilty,

00:02:49.742 --> 00:02:52.340
than if you're poor and innocent.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.104
The opposite of poverty is not wealth.

00:02:55.104 --> 00:02:57.207
The opposite of poverty is justice.

00:02:57.207 --> 00:02:58.378
Try to, try to, try to, try to,

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:00.161
say something, say something,
say something, say something,

00:03:00.161 --> 00:03:02.382
try to, try to, try to, try to,

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:05.731
say something, say something,
say something, say something.

00:03:05.731 --> 00:03:06.564
I've been trying to say something

00:03:06.564 --> 00:03:08.607
about our criminal justice system.

00:03:08.607 --> 00:03:10.764
The United States is the
only country in the world

00:03:10.764 --> 00:03:13.075
where we sentence 13-year-old children,

00:03:13.075 --> 00:03:14.952
where we sentence, sentence, United States

00:03:14.952 --> 00:03:16.676
country in the world, sentence children,

00:03:16.676 --> 00:03:20.102
13-year-old children, the United
States, the United States.

00:03:20.102 --> 00:03:21.822
The United States is the
only country in the world

00:03:21.822 --> 00:03:26.284
where we sentence 13-year-old
children, that die in prison.

00:03:26.284 --> 00:03:28.171
We have life in prison without parole

00:03:28.171 --> 00:03:29.430
for kids in this country.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.597
Only country in the world.

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:35.286
We love innovation; we love technology;

00:03:35.286 --> 00:03:37.786
we love creativity; we love...

00:03:39.232 --> 00:03:43.399
But ultimately, those
realities, realities, realities,

00:03:47.173 --> 00:03:51.006
are shadowed by suffering,
abuse, degradation,

00:03:52.056 --> 00:03:54.085
marginalization, and for me,

00:03:54.085 --> 00:03:56.608
it becomes necessary to integrate the two,

00:03:56.608 --> 00:03:58.690
because ultimately we are
talking about the need

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:01.746
to be more hopeful, more
committed, more dedicated

00:04:01.746 --> 00:04:05.944
to the basic challenges of
living in a complex world.

00:04:05.944 --> 00:04:07.503
To me, that means spending time

00:04:07.503 --> 00:04:12.480
thinking and talking about
the poor, the disadvantaged.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.216
I've come to understand and to believe,

00:04:15.216 --> 00:04:17.302
that each of us is more

00:04:17.302 --> 00:04:19.524
than the worst thing we've ever done.

00:04:19.524 --> 00:04:22.774
(gentle, quirky music)

00:05:05.816 --> 00:05:07.809
- I think the character of any society,

00:05:07.809 --> 00:05:11.237
its commitment to justice, its
commitment to the rule of law

00:05:11.237 --> 00:05:13.712
has to be evaluated not by
looking at how it treats

00:05:13.712 --> 00:05:15.597
the rich and the powerful
and the privileged,

00:05:15.597 --> 00:05:18.135
but by looking at how it treats the poor

00:05:18.135 --> 00:05:20.222
and the incarcerated, the disfavored,

00:05:20.222 --> 00:05:22.393
people living in the margins of society.

00:05:22.393 --> 00:05:25.703
Our response to them,
our relationship to them

00:05:25.703 --> 00:05:26.830
reveals our true character.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:28.662
I think there's something better

00:05:28.662 --> 00:05:31.259
waiting for people in this
country than what we’ve experienced.

00:05:31.259 --> 00:05:34.422
There's something more genuinely free,

00:05:34.422 --> 00:05:37.454
more genuinely equal, more genuinely just,

00:05:37.454 --> 00:05:39.786
than what we've
experienced in this country,

00:05:39.786 --> 00:05:42.258
and if we desire it
and we struggle for it,

00:05:42.258 --> 00:05:43.758
we can achieve it.

